{"file":{"content":"The remaining statement is no longer an operator-theoretic problem. It is a **single-scale (L^2) Fourier/collision estimate** for the renewal constants\n\n[\nC_L=\\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}3^{L-1-j}2^{B_j}\\pmod{3^L}.\n]\n\nI would now stop thinking in terms of individual Fourier coefficients. The levelwise maximum is false for structural reasons, and the dyadic-resonant direction (u/3^L\\to3/4) is the correct warning sign. The proof has to be an **energy proof**:\n\n[\n\\tilde Q_L\n==========\n\n# 3^L\\operatorname{Col}_L(L)\n\n3^L\\Pr[C_L=C_L'\\bmod 3^L]\n\\le \\operatorname{poly}(L).\n]\n\nThe most promising route is exactly the one you propose: digit-conditional collision control. But it should be formulated as a two-word energy cascade, not as pointwise equidistribution.\n\n## 1. Restate the target in its strongest useful form\n\nThe open problem is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\tilde Q_L=3^L\\Pr[C_L=C_L'\\bmod3^L]\\le L^A.\n}\n]\n\nThe numerics suggest the stronger statement:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\tilde Q_L=O(1),\n}\n]\n\npossibly with limiting value near (1.4). But for the finite H23a chain, polynomial is enough.\n\nSo prove polynomial first.\n\nThe false statements are now clear:\n\n[\n\\max_x \\Pr[C_L=x]\\le \\operatorname{poly}(L)3^{-L}\n]\n\nis false because the heavy fiber (x=-1) has mass\n\n[\n\\asymp 2^{-L}.\n]\n\nAnd\n\n[\n\\max_{\\operatorname{lev}(u)=L}|\\hat E_L(u)|^2\\le\\operatorname{poly}(L)3^{-L}\n]\n\nis false because the dyadic-resonant Fourier direction has exponentially larger individual mass.\n\nBut those heavy objects are sparse. The (L^2) aggregate can still be bounded.\n\n## 2. The right proof object: a collision filtration\n\nLet (w,w') be two independent (L)-words with branch law\n\n[\np(b)=2^{-b}.\n]\n\nDefine the collision event\n\n[\nE_L={C_L(w)\\equiv C_L(w')\\pmod{3^L}}.\n]\n\nThe proof should expose the two words digit by digit in the (3)-adic collision condition. The goal is to show that, except for (O(\\log L)) shallow ambiguity, each additional (3)-adic digit of collision costs essentially a factor\n\n[\n\\sum_{b\\ge1}p(b)^2=\\sum_{b\\ge1}4^{-b}=\\frac13.\n]\n\nThus\n\n[\n\\Pr(E_L)\\le \\operatorname{poly}(L)3^{-L}.\n]\n\nThis is the cleanest interpretation of the bounded shells.\n\n## 3. The key local estimate: congruence-class pair mass\n\nAt each digit-extension step, the matching condition should impose a congruence on an increment difference\n\n[\nd=b-b'\n]\n\nmodulo some order\n\n[\nM_k=2\\cdot3^{k-1}.\n]\n\nThe universal pair-weight identity is:\n\n[\n\\Pr[b-b'=d]\n===========\n\n# \\sum_{\\substack{b,b'\\ge1\\b-b'=d}}2^{-b-b'}\n\n\\frac13,2^{-|d|}.\n]\n\nTherefore, for a residue class (r\\bmod M),\n\n[\nA_M(r)\n:=\n\\Pr[b-b'\\equiv r\\bmod M]\n========================\n\n\\frac13\\sum_{d\\equiv r\\bmod M}2^{-|d|}.\n]\n\nThe worst class is (r=0), and\n\n[\nA_M(0)\n======\n\n\\frac13\n\\left(\n1+2\\sum_{n\\ge1}2^{-nM}\n\\right)\n=======\n\n\\frac13\\frac{1+2^{-M}}{1-2^{-M}}.\n]\n\nHence\n\n[\nA_M(r)\n\\le\n\\frac13\\frac{1+2^{-M}}{1-2^{-M}}.\n]\n\nFor\n\n[\nM_k=2\\cdot3^{k-1},\n]\n\nthe excess over (1/3) is doubly-exponentially summable:\n\n[\n\\prod_{k\\ge1}\n\\frac{1+2^{-M_k}}{1-2^{-M_k}}\n<\\infty.\n]\n\nThis is the exact (L^2) analogue of the constant (C_0) that broke the cascade marginality. Depending on the orientation conventions you may get your smaller product\n\n[\n\\prod_{j\\ge0}(1-2^{-2\\cdot3^j})^{-1},\n]\n\nbut the important point is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{the product over levels is finite.}\n}\n]\n\nSo if each new (3)-adic digit of collision imposes **one congruence class** on (b-b'), then\n\n[\n\\Pr(E_L)\n\\le\nC,3^{-L}.\n]\n\nIf the first (O(\\log L)) shallow levels have multiple possible congruence classes, that only costs\n\n[\n3^{O(\\log L)}=\\operatorname{poly}(L).\n]\n\nThis would prove op:flat.\n\n## 4. The central lemma to prove\n\nThe bounded-shell proof should now be reduced to this lemma.\n\n[\n\\textbf{Digit-extension lemma.}\n]\n\nThere exists (K=O(\\log L)) such that, after conditioning on a valid collision through level (k\\ge K), extension to level (k+1) forces\n\n[\nb-b'\\equiv r_k \\pmod{2\\cdot3^{k-1}}\n]\n\nfor a single residue class (r_k) determined by the previously exposed collision state. Equivalently, the conditional pair mass of valid extensions is bounded by\n\n[\n\\frac13\n\\frac{1+2^{-2\\cdot3^{k-1}}}{1-2^{-2\\cdot3^{k-1}}}.\n]\n\nFor (k<K), use the crude bound (1).\n\nThen:\n\n[\n\\Pr(E_L)\n\\le\n3^{-L+K}\\prod_{k\\ge K}\n\\frac{1+2^{-2\\cdot3^{k-1}}}{1-2^{-2\\cdot3^{k-1}}}\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(L)3^{-L}.\n]\n\nThis is the proof in one line, once the digit-extension lemma is established.\n\n## 5. Why this handles the heavy atom\n\nThe heavy atom (C_L=-1) has mass\n\n[\n\\asymp 2^{-L}.\n]\n\nIts contribution to collision energy is\n\n[\n3^L\\cdot 4^{-L}\n===============\n\n\\left(\\frac34\\right)^L,\n]\n\nwhich is exponentially small. More generally, dyadic-resonant atoms are large in (L^\\infty), but their squared masses are small enough when aggregated.\n\nThis is why (L^\\infty) fails while (L^2) succeeds.\n\nThe digit-extension proof automatically accounts for this: large atoms correspond to small (B)-paths, but the pair mass is quadratic,\n\n[\n2^{-B}2^{-B'},\n]\n\nand the congruence-class bound sees the factor\n\n[\n2^{-|b-b'|}.\n]\n\nSo the resonant atoms do not need to be separately excluded unless they complicate the digit-extension lemma. They are already paid for in energy.\n\n## 6. How to formulate the collision state\n\nThe only delicate part is choosing the right filtration.\n\nA naive prefix filtration on\n\n[\nC_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}3^{k-1-j}2^{B_j}\n]\n\nmay not be the correct one because threshold coupling occurs at full level. Use the suffix/window form instead.\n\nFor level (k), write the terminal (k)-window variable\n\n[\nX_k(w)\n======\n\n\\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}3^{k-1-i}\n2^{B_{L-k+i}-B_{L-k}}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\nC_L(w)\\bmod3^k\n==============\n\n2^{B_{L-k}(w)}X_k(w).\n]\n\nFor a pair ((w,w')), define the level-(k) collision state:\n\n[\nE_k:\n\\quad\n2^{B_{L-k}(w)}X_k(w)\n\\equiv\n2^{B_{L-k}(w')}X_k(w')\n\\pmod{3^k}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\nE_L\n]\n\nis the desired collision. Moving from (k) to (k+1) adds one new symbol on the left of the terminal window. The new condition should yield exactly the congruence\n\n[\nb-b'\\equiv r_k\\pmod{2\\cdot3^{k-1}},\n]\n\nup to shallow ambiguity.\n\nThis is the precise place where the proof lives.\n\n## 7. The shallow ambiguity\n\nFor small (k), the modulus\n\n[\n2\\cdot3^{k-1}\n]\n\nis not large compared with typical partial sums, and several congruence representatives may contribute non-negligibly.\n\nDo not try to optimize this. Choose\n\n[\nK=\\left\\lceil A\\log_3 L\\right\\rceil\n]\n\nlarge enough that for (k\\ge K), all nonzero representatives\n\n[\nr+n(2\\cdot3^{k-1})\n]\n\nwith (n\\ne0) have pair mass\n\n[\n\\ll L^{-B}\n]\n\nafter summing over all future levels. Then the early (K) levels cost at most\n\n[\n3^K=L^A.\n]\n\nThat is exactly why a polynomial loss is natural.\n\nThis gives a robust proof even if the digit-extension condition is a union of (O(1)) congruence classes for shallow (k).\n\n## 8. The proof skeleton\n\nHere is the full bounded-shell proof as I would write it.\n\n### Step 1: Terminal-window collision filtration\n\nDefine (E_k) as above. Then\n\n[\nE_L={C_L=C_L'\\bmod3^L}.\n]\n\n### Step 2: Digit-extension congruence\n\nProve:\n\n[\nE_{k+1}\\cap E_k\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nb_{L-k-1}-b'_{L-k-1}\n\\equiv r_k\n\\pmod{2\\cdot3^{k-1}},\n]\n\nwhere (r_k) depends on the already-exposed terminal windows and prefix-unit ratio.\n\nThis is where you use:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{ord}_{3^k}(2)=2\\cdot3^{k-1}.\n]\n\n### Step 3: Pair-mass bound\n\nFor every residue (r\\bmod M_k),\n\n[\n\\Pr[b-b'\\equiv r\\bmod M_k]\n\\le\n\\frac13\n\\frac{1+2^{-M_k}}{1-2^{-M_k}}.\n]\n\n### Step 4: Product estimate\n\nTherefore\n\n[\n\\Pr(E_L)\n\\le\n\\Pr(E_K)\n\\prod_{k=K}^{L-1}\n\\frac13\n\\frac{1+2^{-M_k}}{1-2^{-M_k}}.\n]\n\nSince\n\n[\n\\Pr(E_K)\\le1\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n\\prod_{k\\ge K}\n\\frac{1+2^{-M_k}}{1-2^{-M_k}}\n\\le C,\n]\n\nwe get\n\n[\n\\Pr(E_L)\\le C3^{-(L-K)}.\n]\n\nWith\n\n[\nK=O(\\log L),\n]\n\nthis gives\n\n[\n\\Pr(E_L)\\le \\operatorname{poly}(L)3^{-L}.\n]\n\nThus\n\n[\n\\tilde Q_L=3^L\\Pr(E_L)\\le\\operatorname{poly}(L).\n]\n\nThis proves op:flat.\n\n## 9. What can go wrong?\n\nThere are two possible failure modes.\n\n### Failure mode 1: extension condition is not a single congruence class\n\nIf (E_{k+1}) allows many residue classes for (b-b'), then you need to bound the number of classes.\n\nIt is enough to prove:\n\n[\n#{\\text{allowed classes at level }k}\\le \\operatorname{poly}(L)\n]\n\noverall, or preferably (O(1)) per shallow level and (1) for (k\\ge K).\n\nThe numerics suggest the latter.\n\n### Failure mode 2: conditioning on (E_k) biases the distribution of the next symbols\n\nThis should not happen if the filtration exposes the terminal window from right to left and the next symbols are fresh independent increments. That is why the terminal-window filtration is important.\n\nDo not expose from the wrong end. If the new symbols are not fresh under the conditioning, the argument breaks.\n\nSo the proof must be set up so that, after conditioning on the already-revealed terminal windows, the next pair\n\n[\n(b,b')\n]\n\nis independent with law\n\n[\n2^{-b}2^{-b'}.\n]\n\nThat is the main reason to use suffix windows.\n\n## 10. Numerical test for the digit-extension lemma\n\nBefore writing the proof, run a direct audit:\n\nFor (L=6,7,8), for each level (k), condition on sampled pairs satisfying (E_k). For those that also satisfy (E_{k+1}), compute\n\n[\nd=b_{L-k-1}-b'_{L-k-1}\n\\pmod{2\\cdot3^{k-1}}.\n]\n\nVerify:\n\n1. for fixed collision state, there is exactly one allowed residue class;\n2. the empirical conditional mass of (E_{k+1}\\mid E_k) matches\n[\nA_{M_k}(r_k);\n]\n3. the worst conditional factor approaches (1/3) after shallow levels;\n4. the product of observed excess factors stays bounded.\n\nThis will confirm the proof architecture.\n\n## 11. Manuscript endpoint\n\nThe paper should now say:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Open Problem op:flat}\n]\n\nis equivalent to proving the terminal-window digit-extension lemma and its pair-mass product bound.\n\nI would add a short “Candidate proof of op:flat” subsection after the open problem:\n\n[\n\\text{terminal-window filtration}\n\\to\n\\text{one congruence class per digit}\n\\to\n\\text{geometric pair-mass bound}\n\\to\n\\operatorname{poly}(L)3^{-L}.\n]\n\nThis gives readers a clear next theorem rather than just an open Fourier estimate.\n\n## 12. Bottom line\n\nThe bounded-shell proof should not try to control individual fibers or individual Fourier coefficients. Both are false.\n\nIt should prove:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{each additional }3\\text{-adic digit of collision costs }1/3\n\t\\text{ in pair energy, up to summable dyadic wrap corrections.}\n}\n]\n\nThe local computation is:\n\n[\n\\Pr[b-b'\\equiv r\\bmod 2\\cdot3^{k-1}]\n\\le\n\\frac13(1+O(2^{-2\\cdot3^{k-1}})).\n]\n\nThe global conclusion is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\tilde Q_L=3^L\\operatorname{Col}_L(L)\\le\\operatorname{poly}(L).\n}\n]\n\nThat is the cleanest route to op:flat, and therefore to the finite H23a operator theorem.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_26.md","numLines":574,"startLine":1,"totalLines":574},"type":"text"}